  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v=\\frac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(u+v)=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}\u2061u+{cos}^{2}\u2061u=1\\,\u27f9\\,{sin}^{2}\u2061u=1-{cos}^{2}\u2061u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061u & =1-{cos}^{2}\u2061u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{24}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u=\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{24}{25})-(\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{7}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{65}-\\frac{84}{325} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{325}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
